            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount && New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new count is equal to old count plus one after insertion 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x in new array is equal to index in old array plus one after insertion 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index + 1);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is equal to last index in old array plus one after insertion 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ contains of new array is true after insertion 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == true);
        }        
    }
}

```

I've also created a GitHub issue for it: https://github.com/microsoft/vstest/issues/655

Please note that `PexObserve`, `Debug.Assert`, `PexAssumeUnderTest` and `AssumePrecondition` are just place holders as I'm not sure how they can be included in the PexRunner and the real testing context.
Testing context matters as ArrayLists behave differently in different environments and certain methods can throw exceptions depending on the content of the array. So the tests should be run in a controlled environment that simulates this behavior. So your team needs to implement some sort of PexRunner that can effectively run these tests in a controlled environment. 

There is a `TestClass` in the context of MSTest and `PexClass` in the context of NUnit.
   * PexClass allows Pex/Moles to work within a test and provide a controlled environment for the tests.


  *Pex/Moles use Microsoft Fakes Framework under the hood. Given the capabilities of this framework, you may encounter issues, bugs or limitations depending on the complexity of the code being tested.*

   So it's highly advised to test these methods manually or in the controlled unit testing environment to verify their correctness before considering automated testing.


   I would suggest using a combination of manual unit tests and automated tests - i.e., testing individual methods with automated testing and test everything else manually.


   Note: This solution is not ideal and won't provide the best approach in the real world. Automated tools are very useful and all, but they are meant to be used with understanding of the principles upon which they rely and being mindful of the potential limitations and risks. Unit testing alone may not suffice in many scenarios if you build larger, more complex systems.

   Another thing to consider is the time it takes to run tests. These unit tests could be very slow if not handled correctly. The time to create and destroy unit tests increases significantly as the size of the classes and methods increases. The faster the tests, the better.

   Another thing to consider is the quality of the system under test. In the real world, you can never predict how the system under test will behave. So the goal is to write very predictive tests that fail loudly (and unpredictably) if the system does not behave as expected.
 
   Good luck with your code. Make sure to validate on the actual system/environment. Automated tests are not a silver bullet but a supporting tool or another type of assistant in a broader picture approach. The quality of code can be much more than the sum of the part written by automated test code.   


A: I have used ArrayList.Test and found it to be quite versatile. It is a manual and automatically generated unit test.
  
  
*
  
*Automatic testing: ArrayList.Test does not generate automated unit tests automatically. You’re on your own to set that up or you might need some assistance. The main reason is that I’m not sure if there’s any automated testing library which does the complex method like 'Insert' in the list in your specific use-case. There are no testing libraries in C# that provide automation unit testing for complex methods like the ArrayList.Test.InsertContract().
  
*Manual Testing: You might write some unit tests manually, but that would result in a large amount of code and is--------------------
        }
    }
}
```

## <a name="conclusion"></a>结论
Pex 中的 Assumptions 和 Observe 可以帮助我们更快地找出任何可能存在的系统行为问题。 当我们有一个 ArrayList 类时，这个 ArrayList 类可以接受任意整数值和任意索引插入，而 Pex 可以提供可测试的参数。 从 Pex 返回的测试结果可以验证类是否符合预期，并可以指示如果测试失败需要改进的地方。 对于 Pex 很难想象的事情，这应该是有力的证明。

虽然 Pex 可以自动为你生成很多有趣的行为，但这需要花费一些时间，你需要验证生成的测试用例是否有意义，并根据需要进行调整。 需要注意的是，Pex 并不是万能的，对于某些问题它无法找到满意的测试参数或根据其随机迭代，可能会返回有问题的测试用例，这取决于问题的性质。

总之，Pex 提供了一个强大的工具，可以自动为你检查代码的各种可能行为，这使它成为一种非常有益的探索工具，特别适用于更大范围的问题和更加复杂的场景。

